lesson:
```
if (temp < 2) {
    System.out.println("Too cold");
} else if (temp <= 8) {
    System.out.println("OK");
} else {
    System.out.println("Too warm");
}
```

The condition goes in round brackets and must be a `boolean`. Comparisons: `==` `!=` `<` `>` `<=` `>=` (for numbers and chars; Strings use `.equals`). Combine with `&&` (and), `||` (or), `!` (not). Only the first true branch runs, so check the most specific case first.

Braces are optional for a single line, but always use them: leaving them out is a classic source of bugs when someone adds a second line later.

**switch** picks among fixed values, neatly. Modern Java (14+) has the arrow form:

```
String day = switch (n) {
    case 1 -> "Monday";
    case 2 -> "Tuesday";
    default -> "Another day";
};
```

The **ternary** operator is a one-line if for values: `String size = kg > 100 ? "big" : "small";`

example.code:
public class Example {
    static String fridge(double t) {
        if (t < 2) return "too cold";
        else if (t <= 8) return "ok";
        else return "too warm";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(fridge(5) + " " + fridge(-1) + " " + fridge(9.5));
        int n = 2;
        String day = switch (n) {
            case 1 -> "Monday";
            case 2 -> "Tuesday";
            default -> "Another day";
        };
        System.out.println(day);
        System.out.println(40 > 100 ? "big" : "small");
    }
}

